WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.281
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book one.

2
00:00:05.581 --> 00:00:09.246
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.546 --> 00:00:15.971
Unit nineteen: Scholarly Collaboration. Today you will attribute responsibility with the passive and a by-agent.

4
00:00:16.271 --> 00:00:18.784
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.733 --> 00:00:23.291
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:25.395
Co-authorship.

7
00:00:26.383 --> 00:00:29.683
Co-authorship means shared authorship of a publication.

8
00:00:30.283 --> 00:00:33.618
Co-authorship should reflect a substantial contribution.

9
00:00:34.217 --> 00:00:37.345
Your turn. Say: Co-authorship.

10
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.176
Co-authorship.

11
00:00:42.917 --> 00:00:44.173
Collaboration.

12
00:00:45.183 --> 00:00:48.866
Collaboration means cooperative work towards a shared research goal.

13
00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:52.471
International collaboration accelerates discovery.

14
00:00:53.083 --> 00:00:55.689
Your turn. Say: Collaboration.

15
00:00:58.533 --> 00:00:59.743
Collaboration.

16
00:01:00.483 --> 00:01:01.624
Contribution.

17
00:01:02.617 --> 00:01:06.404
Contribution means the specific input each person makes to a project.

18
00:01:07.017 --> 00:01:09.957
Contribution statements list each author's role.

19
00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:13.423
Your turn. Say: Contribution.

20
00:01:16.267 --> 00:01:17.523
Contribution.

21
00:01:18.283 --> 00:01:19.482
Consensus.

22
00:01:20.483 --> 00:01:23.644
Consensus means general agreement among all members.

23
00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:27.051
Authorship order was decided by consensus.

24
00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:30.430
Your turn. Say: Consensus.

25
00:01:33.267 --> 00:01:34.372
Consensus.

26
00:01:35.117 --> 00:01:36.385
Division of labor.

27
00:01:37.383 --> 00:01:41.531
Division of labor means the allocation of tasks according to skills.

28
00:01:42.117 --> 00:01:44.917
A clear division of labor prevented duplication.

29
00:01:45.517 --> 00:01:48.378
Your turn. Say: Division of labor.

30
00:01:51.233 --> 00:01:52.827
Division of labor.

31
00:01:53.583 --> 00:01:54.329
Credit.

32
00:01:55.317 --> 00:01:57.978
Credit means public acknowledgement of someone's work.

33
00:01:58.583 --> 00:02:01.686
Junior researchers often receive too little credit.

34
00:02:02.283 --> 00:02:04.924
Your turn. Say: Credit.

35
00:02:07.767 --> 00:02:08.640
Credit.

36
00:02:09.383 --> 00:02:10.408
Partnership.

37
00:02:11.417 --> 00:02:16.551
Partnership means a cooperative relationship between institutions or individuals.

38
00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:20.566
The partnership linked universities in Cambodia and Japan.

39
00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:23.860
Your turn. Say: Partnership.

40
00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:27.724
Partnership.

41
00:02:28.483 --> 00:02:29.403
Synergy.

42
00:02:30.417 --> 00:02:34.216
Synergy means combined effort producing more than the sum of its parts.

43
00:02:34.817 --> 00:02:37.524
The synergy between the two labs was remarkable.

44
00:02:38.117 --> 00:02:40.502
Your turn. Say: Synergy.

45
00:02:43.367 --> 00:02:44.461
Synergy.

46
00:02:45.267 --> 00:02:48.894
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:54.457
Visal, your team's report lists five authors. How was the order decided?

48
00:02:55.550 --> 00:03:02.646
Credit should be divided fairly by all contributors, so we agreed on criteria before writing a single word.

49
00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:05.285
What were the criteria?

50
00:03:06.383 --> 00:03:14.246
The research design was proposed by Dara, and most of the analysis was carried out by me, so we shared first authorship.

51
00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:17.036
Were there any disagreements?

52
00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:25.818
One. It was resolved by our supervisor, who reminded us that collecting data is as valuable as writing.

53
00:03:26.967 --> 00:03:28.688
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:32.455
Grammar focus: Passive with by-agent.

55
00:03:32.755 --> 00:03:33.849
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:34.550 --> 00:03:37.571
Credit should be divided fairly by all contributors.

57
00:03:38.483 --> 00:03:41.075
The research design was proposed by Dara.

58
00:03:41.983 --> 00:03:44.563
The disagreement was resolved by our supervisor.

59
00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:54.956
Include the by-agent only when the agent is important or new information. In collaboration, naming who did what is essential, so the by-agent is often needed.

60
00:03:55.717 --> 00:03:59.820
Be careful! Don't say: The data was collected from Dara.

61
00:04:01.767 --> 00:04:04.649
Say: The data was collected by Dara.

62
00:04:04.949 --> 00:04:06.948
Use 'by' to introduce the agent.

63
00:04:07.733 --> 00:04:10.362
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:11.167 --> 00:04:13.015
Sharing the Work, Sharing the Credit.

65
00:04:13.315 --> 00:04:17.602
Modern research is increasingly conducted by large, international teams.

66
00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:24.849
In such projects, credit should be divided fairly by all contributors, ideally before the work begins.

67
00:04:25.833 --> 00:04:31.397
Many journals now require contribution statements, in which each author's role is described explicitly.

68
00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:37.116
Disputes are frequently caused by unspoken assumptions about who deserves recognition.

69
00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:44.221
When tasks are allocated transparently, collaboration produces genuine synergy rather than resentment.

70
00:04:45.333 --> 00:04:49.660
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:50.517 --> 00:04:53.573
Credit should be divided fairly blank all contributors.

72
00:04:53.873 --> 00:04:56.818
A, from. B, by. C, with.

73
00:05:00.367 --> 00:05:02.250
The answer is B: by.

74
00:05:03.417 --> 00:05:05.161
The analysis blank by me.

75
00:05:05.461 --> 00:05:09.857
A, was carried out. B, carried out. C, was carrying out.

76
00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:15.678
The answer is A: was carried out.

77
00:05:16.817 --> 00:05:19.199
General agreement among all members is blank.

78
00:05:19.499 --> 00:05:23.571
A, synergy. B, consensus. C, credit.

79
00:05:27.133 --> 00:05:29.725
The answer is B: consensus.

80
00:05:30.883 --> 00:05:32.732
When should the by-agent be included?

81
00:05:33.032 --> 00:05:38.566
A, always. B, when the agent is important. C, never in academic writing.

82
00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:44.901
The answer is B: when the agent is important.

83
00:05:46.050 --> 00:05:49.327
According to the reading, what often causes authorship disputes?

84
00:05:49.627 --> 00:05:56.113
A, unspoken assumptions. B, contribution statements. C, large teams.

85
00:05:59.667 --> 00:06:02.514
The answer is A: unspoken assumptions.

86
00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:06.933
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:07.783 --> 00:06:10.955
How should credit be divided fairly among co-authors?

88
00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:29.436
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:29.736 --> 00:07:03.453
I believe credit should be agreed on by the whole team before the project begins, not after. Each person's contribution should be described explicitly, for example who designed the study, who collected the data and who wrote the paper. This prevents the common situation in which junior researchers are overlooked. Some argue that the senior researcher should always be listed first, since they secured the funding. However, I think authorship should reflect actual work rather than status.

90
00:07:03.753 --> 00:07:05.381
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:06.283 --> 00:07:10.538
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and passive with by-agent.

92
00:07:10.838 --> 00:07:15.406
Today's value is fairness. Look for solutions that are fair to everyone.

93
00:07:15.706 --> 00:07:18.521
See you in Unit twenty. Goodbye!

94
00:07:18.821 --> 00:07:21.949
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

